In Unity, when you instantiate a GameObject (e.g., via `Instantiate`), it appears at the root of the scene hierarchy by default. To place it under a specific "folder" (i.e., a parent GameObject for organization), set its parent after instantiation.

Assuming you have a reference to the parent Transform (e.g., a folder GameObject in the scene), here's how:

```csharp
// Example: Instantiate a prefab and set its parent
public Transform parentFolder;  // Assign this in the Inspector to your desired parent GameObject

void InstantiateInFolder(GameObject prefab) {
    GameObject newObj = Instantiate(prefab);
    newObj.transform.SetParent(parentFolder);  // Places it under the parent
    // Optional: Reset local position/rotation if needed
    newObj.transform.localPosition = Vector3.zero;
    newObj.transform.localRotation = Quaternion.identity;
}
```

- **Why this works**: `SetParent` moves the object in the hierarchy without affecting world position/rotation (unless you specify `worldPositionStays = false`).
- **If the parent doesn't exist yet**: Create it first, e.g., `GameObject folder = new GameObject("MyFolder"); parentFolder = folder.transform;`
- **For chunks**: In your `ChunkLoader.cs`, you can do this after generating each chunk to keep the hierarchy organized (e.g., all chunks under a "Chunks" parent).

If you meant something else by "folder" (like a project folder or asset organization), let me know for more details!

